    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Throughout the US, student athletes, especially those who are aspiring to represent the US at the summer Olympics or become professional athletes, struggle to balance athletic training with academic pursuits. So focused are they on physical conditioning, practice, and tournaments that they neglect their academics, often resulting in weak academic foundations and precluding their success on standardized tests, a requirement for admission and scholarships to prep-schools and colleges. Without scholarships, many student athletes are unable to afford college. Regardless of whether these athletes ultimately pursue a career in sports, excelling on standardized tests opens up a wider array of career opportunities. \r\n\r\nResearch shows that the refined ability to manage time, prioritize, and apply mastery outside of their sport distinguishes student athletes who successfully balance academics and athletics from other athletes.  Given these findings, high school athletes need a supportive mobile community of peers who prioritize honing these skills such that they can transfer mastery techniques developed in their sports to a target area, namely standardized tests, and improve their long-term professional outcomes.\r\n\r\nEnter Kandodis, a mobile application with three key components: Journey, a series of lessons on time management, prioritization, and mastery; Projects, a tool to plan, execute, and curate project experiences; and Storyboard, a platform to share experiences and motivational videos. Having tested high fidelity wire-frame prototypes, I have confirmed the value to the learner; however, testing the actual mobile app is needed to validate the design herein described and measure the projected improvement in academic performance.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
